## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Take a Trip to the Panama Canal</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize articles for classroom discussions. Concept: Explain cultural differences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain cultural differences by using pictures of traditional artifacts and gestures (e.g., points to a handmade Ngäbe-Buglé chácara and explains how it is woven by hand, holds up a Guna Yala mola and describes its vibrant patterns and stories, or shows an Emberá basket and explains how it is crafted from natural fibers like palm leaves). Can explain Indigenous cultural differences using visuals and gestures (e.g., shows a picture of traditional clothing and gestures to highlight its colors). Can summarize key points from an article about indigenous cultures to help peers understand important traditions and practices during classroom discussions (e.g., creating a mind map about how the Naso Tjër Di celebrate their festival).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a picture of the Panama Canal, specifically the Miraflores Locks. The teacher asks, "What do you see in this picture?" "What is the Panama Canal famous for?" "Why do tourists visit this historical site?" Students briefly share their ideas, activating prior knowledge on historical sites and tourism. The teacher then quickly introduces the idea of explaining this landmark to someone who has never heard of it, setting the stage for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing the Panama Canal's importance, for example: "The Panama Canal is a renowned historical site. It connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans, allowing ships to transit quickly. Many visitors come to learn about its history and engineering. It's a key part of Panama's heritage." The teacher reads the summary aloud, modeling clear pronunciation, focusing on stress in words like "historical," "information," and "cultural." The teacher then gestures as if explaining to someone, pointing to keywords and rephrasing for clarity, demonstrating how to mediate information simply and effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'summarize' and 'explain'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I summarize an article, do I tell every single detail or just the main ideas?" (Expected answer: Just the main ideas.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain cultural differences, should I just say 'different' or give examples and use gestures?" (Expected answer: Give examples and use gestures.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational article (approx. 150 words) titled "Panama's Indigenous Cultures: A Rich Heritage" with accompanying pictures of traditional artifacts (e.g., a Ngäbe-Buglé *chácara*, a Guna Yala *mola*, an Emberá basket, traditional clothing). The article introduces how indigenous cultures are also part of Panama's tourism, mentioning the craftsmanship and traditions. It uses vocabulary such as *heritage*, *artifacts*, *traditional*, *craft*, *patterns*, *stories*, *visitors*, *explore* and grammatical structures like present simple for descriptions and simple past for historical context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding intent and context by identifying key information and preparing to explain cultural aspects to others. Students will also practice pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "cultural," "historical," and "information."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the article individually. Then, in pairs, they answer gist questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea of this article?" (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the text, what are some traditional artifacts mentioned, and which indigenous groups make them?" (Specific information)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do tourists find these cultures attractive?" (Inferential)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice summarizing key information and explaining cultural differences, using present simple for descriptions, simple past for historical facts, and possessive forms ('s). They will use vocabulary related to historical sites and cultural artifacts (e.g., *heritage*, *artifacts*, *patterns*, *stories*, *monument*, *visitor*, *preserve*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill Summary:</b> The teacher provides a partially completed summary of the "Panama's Indigenous Cultures" article. Students work individually to fill in the blanks using words from a provided word bank (e.g., *heritage, patterns, visitors, explain, cultural, historical*). This reinforces key vocabulary and the structure of a summary. Example: "Panama's rich indigenous ______ is very attractive to ______. They can see beautiful ______ on Guna Yala molas and learn how Ngäbe-Buglé chácaras are woven. These groups ______ their unique traditions."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Explain with Visuals and Gestures:</b> In pairs, students receive a different picture of a Panamanian indigenous artifact or traditional clothing (e.g., a *chácara*, *mola*, *Emberá basket*). Partner A describes the artifact using vocabulary from the article and gestures to emphasize features (e.g., points to a *chácara* and explains, "This is a Ngäbe-Buglé *chácara*. It is woven by hand from natural fibers. It was used for carrying things."). Partner B then summarizes Partner A's explanation to a third imaginary person. They switch roles. This directly addresses the learning outcome of explaining cultural differences using visuals and gestures. The teacher monitors for correct use of present simple and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Information Exchange:</b> Students work in groups of four. Each student chooses one paragraph or section from the article about indigenous cultures. They create 2-3 bullet points summarizing their section. Then, each student takes a turn to *mediate* their summary to their group, explaining the main ideas and important traditions, making sure their peers understand. Other group members ask questions for clarification, and the presenter rephrases or adds detail if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation – "Our Historical Site Video Documentary Pitch"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their project groups to prepare a brief "pitch" (presentation) for their "Historical Site Video Documentary" project. Each group will select a historical site (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo, or an indigenous community's significant cultural site if relevant to their project) and present a summary of their video's content and their plans for filming. The task is to *mediate* their project idea to the class, making it clear and interesting for their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group prepares a 2-3 minute presentation. They must summarize the key information about their chosen historical site, explain why it is important, and outline what they *are going to do* in their video documentary (e.g., "We are going to visit the Panama Canal next month." "We are going to interview a local guide.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should incorporate target vocabulary (e.g., *historical site, monument, heritage, visitor, explore, preserve*) and grammar (present simple, simple past for historical context, "going to" for future plans, possessive 's) naturally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should focus on clearly explaining their ideas, using gestures or visuals (if prepared) to enhance their mediation. For example, if their documentary involves indigenous culture, they should explain specific cultural differences as per the learning outcome.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During presentations, other students listen and can ask one clarifying question at the end, which the presenting group must mediate a clear answer to.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess each group's "Historical Site Video Documentary Pitch" (from Stage 4) based on their ability to mediate information effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarized key information about the historical site clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used "going to" correctly for video plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporated relevant vocabulary (e.g., *historical sites, destination, preserve, heritage*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explained cultural differences using examples and gestures (if applicable to their project).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation was generally intelligible (minimal pairs, word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responded clearly to peer questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and clear communication within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about mediating information today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now summarize and explain ______ about historical sites/cultures."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining or summarizing was easiest for me, and what was most challenging?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will improving my mediation skills help me in my everyday life or in my 'Historical Site Video Documentary' project?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., Students actively used visuals and gestures to explain cultural artifacts; the project pitch motivated them to summarize clearly.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or theme? (e.g., Provide more sentence starters for explaining cultural differences; dedicate more time to peer feedback on summaries; focus on specific pronunciation challenges like consonant clusters in complex vocabulary.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Connection to next lesson/theme: This lesson concludes the "Historical Sites and Tourism" theme. The next theme will build on communicative skills by introducing new vocabulary and grammar in a different context, perhaps related to "Panama's Biodiversity," which will require students to transfer their mediation skills to new content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>